    "updated_at": "2025-09-29T11:23:25Z",
    "body": "> Yes that's the other way to restore scroll position. Either automatically when the promises resolve, or manually by calling ‘scroll()‘. Both of these are in the spec and the test link in the OP seems to check the latter.\n\nRight, this is what is confusing me. AIUI that Chrome defers the results of calling `.scroll()` until intercepts resolve. So if you manually call `.scroll()` the spec, to me, reads as if the restoration is instant, but Chrome behaves differently to that. I cobbled together something which demonstrates this issue from one of the tests:\n\n\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<body>\n  <div id=\"log\" popover style=\"white-space: pre\"></div>\n  <div style=\"height: 200vh; width: 200vw; background: blue\"></div>\n  <div id=\"frag\" style=\"background: green; height: 100px\"></div>\n  <script>\n    const sleep = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));\n    (async t => {\n      log.showPopover();\n      await sleep(1000);\n      await navigation.navigate(\"#frag\").finished;\n      let intercept_resolve;\n      let navigate_event;\n      navigation.onnavigate = e => {\n        navigate_event = e;\n        log.textContent += `navigation!!: Interceiping ${window.scrollY} != 0\\n`;\n        e.intercept({\n          handler: () => new Promise(r => (intercept_resolve = r)),\n          scroll: \"manual\",\n        });\n      };\n      let back_promises = navigation.back();\n      await back_promises.committed;\n      log.textContent += `Step 1: before scroll() ${window.scrollY} == 0\\n`;\n      navigate_event.scroll();\n      log.textContent += `Step 2: after scroll() ${window.scrollY} == 0\\n`;\n      await sleep(10000);\n      log.textContent += `Step 3: after scroll() + 10ms ${window.scrollY} == 0\\n`;\n      intercept_resolve();\n      log.textContent += `Step 4: intercept_resolve: scroll() ${window.scrollY} == 0\\n`;\n      await sleep(1000);\n      await back_promises.finished;\n      log.textContent += `Step 5: back finished ${window.scrollY} == 0\\n`;\n    })();\n  </script>\n</body>\n```\n\nAs you can see `Step 2: after scroll()` occurs after calling `navigate_event.scroll()`. As I read the spec, this should cause the scroll to the top of the page to be committed, and `scrollY` should be `0`, but at that point in time Chrome gives me non-zero:\n\n<img width=\"1205\" height=\"1094\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6679b24b-160b-4788-b840-6d256972b28b\" />\n\nDoes this help explain the problem? (Yes I know there are terrible typos in this demo, interceiping is not a word, but let's all pretend like we didn't see that...)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
